1
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
MOBY-DICK; or, THE WHALE. By Herman Melville.

2
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
ETYMOLOGY.

3
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:29,500
(Supplied by a Late Consumptive Usher to a Grammar School.)

4
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:37,000
The pale Usher—threadbare in coat, heart, body, and brain; I see him now.

5
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:42,000
He was ever dusting his old lexicons and grammars, with a queer handkerchief,

6
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:47,500
mockingly embellished with all the gay flags of all the known nations of the world.

7
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:55,000
He loved to dust his old grammars; it somehow mildly reminded him of his mortality.

8
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:03,000
“While you take in hand to school others, and to teach them by what name a whale-fish is to be called in our tongue,

9
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:10,000
leaving out, through ignorance, the letter H, which almost alone maketh up the signification of the word,

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:17,000
you deliver that which is not true.” —Hackluyt.

11
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:25,500
“WHALE. * * * Sw. and Dan. hval. This animal is named from roundness or rolling;

12
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:34,000
for in Dan. hvalt is arched or vaulted.” —Webster’s Dictionary.

13
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:42,000
“WHALE. * * * It is more immediately from the Dut. and Ger. Wallen;

14
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:51,000
A.S. Walw-ian, to roll, to wallow.” —Richardson’s Dictionary.
